---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import TopicList from '../../../components/overview/TopicList.astro';
import {
  DRONE_FAILSAFE_BASE,
  DRONE_FAILSAFE_SOURCE,
  droneFailsafeParts,
} from '../../../components/tutorials/tutorials';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../../../consts';

const themes = { light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' } as const;
const source = `${GITHUB_URL}/tree/main/${DRONE_FAILSAFE_SOURCE}/part_2_dynamic_context`;

const run = `cargo run -p dynamic_drone_failsafe --example drone_failsafe_part_2`;

const contextType = `/// The ground below the drone as the controller believes it to be. Each patch the camera and
/// LiDAR have seen is a space node linked to five uncertain data nodes, one per measured quantity.
/// A clock node holds the local time of day.
pub type GroundContext = Context<
    UncertainData<FloatType>,
    EuclideanSpace<FloatType>,
    DiscreteTime,
    NoSpaceTime<FloatType>,
>;`;

const fuse = `if first_sight {
    let (x, y) = reading.centre();
    let space_id = node_id(patch, 0);
    let space = context.add_node(Contextoid::new(
        space_id,
        ContextoidType::Spaceoid(
          EuclideanSpace::new(space_id, x, y, ZERO)
          ),
    ))?;
    for q in Quantity::ALL {
        let id = node_id(patch, q as u64 + 1);
        let data = context.add_node(data_node(id, &fused, q))?;
        context.add_edge(space, data, RelationKind::Spatial)?;
    }
} else {
    for q in Quantity::ALL {
        let id = node_id(patch, q as u64 + 1);
        context.update_node(id, data_node(id, &fused, q))?;
    }
}`;

const dataNode = `/// A data node holding the fused value of one quantity as a normal distribution.
fn data_node(id: ContextoidId, fusion: &Fusion, q: Quantity) -> GroundNode {
    let k = q as usize;
    let mean = fusion.weighted[k] / fusion.precision[k];
    let sigma = Real::sqrt(ONE / fusion.precision[k]);
    Contextoid::new(
        id,
        ContextoidType::Datoid(
            UncertainData::new(id, Uncertain::normal(mean, sigma))
          ),
    )
}`;

const stages = `process = process
   .bind(|_, state, ctx| model::sense(frame, state, ctx))
   .bind(model::perceive)
   .bind(model::judge)
   .bind(|value, state, ctx| model::detect(value, state, ctx, &detectors))
   .bind(|value, state, ctx| model::decide(value, state, ctx, time_s));`;

const judge = `let read = |q: Quantity| belief(context, patch, q);
let steep = read(Quantity::Slope)?.greater_than(TIP_OVER_DEG);
let no_return = read(Quantity::Returns)?.less_than(NO_RETURN_BELOW);
let contrast = if night {
    read(Quantity::MeanTemperature)?.greater_than(land_c + WATER_CONTRAST_C)
} else {
    read(Quantity::MeanTemperature)?.less_than(land_c - WATER_CONTRAST_C)
};
let water = no_return.clone() & contrast;
// The LiDAR measures a bump only where it has a return.
let person = read(Quantity::HotSpot)?.greater_than(land_c + PERSON_EXCESS_C)
    | (!no_return.clone() & read(Quantity::Protrusion)?.greater_than(PERSON_BUMP_M));

if holds(&!(steep.clone() | no_return | person.clone()))? {
    return Ok(Ground::Safe);
}`;

const holds = `fn holds(condition: &UncertainBool<FloatType>) -> Result<bool, CausalityError> {
    condition
        .probability_exceeds(
            &SampleSession::seeded(JUDGE_SEED),
            CONFIDENCE,
            CONFIDENCE,
            TEST_EPSILON,
            MAX_SAMPLES,
        )
        .map_err(uncertain_error)
}`;

const output = `Dynamic drone fail-safe, part 2: dynamic context
The same drone flies the same night. A thermal camera and a LiDAR now look at the ground below it.
The column "below" is the controller's judgement of the ground directly under the drone.

 time   across   along   agl  sats  hdop  loss   cell  confirmed faults                       below        fail-safe
   0s      60m      0m   40m    14   0.8    2%  4.05V  none                                   too steep    continue
  18s      60m    144m   40m    14   0.8    2%  4.01V  none                                   safe         continue
  20s      60m    160m   40m    14   0.8    2%  4.01V  none                                   too steep    continue
  50s      60m    400m   40m     7   3.5    2%  3.95V  fix degraded                           too steep    continue
  57s      56m    440m   40m     3  99.0    2%  3.94V  fix lost                               too steep    hold
  68s      34m    440m   40m     3  99.0   95%  3.91V  fix lost                               safe         hold
  69s      32m    440m   40m     3  99.0   95%  3.91V  fix lost, link lost                    safe         land now
  74s      22m    440m   32m     3  99.0   95%  3.90V  fix lost, link lost                    too steep    land now
  80s      10m    440m   24m     3  99.0   95%  3.89V  fix lost, link lost                    water        land now
  85s       0m    440m   16m     3  99.0   95%  3.25V  fix lost, link lost, battery critical  water        land now

Touchdown at 91 s, 440 m along the line, 71 m from the nearest person.
It dropped into the creek and was lost.

The ground as the controller judged it, one 4 m patch per cell, downhill to the left:

              ~ ~ / / / . . . / / / / / / / / /
          ~ ~ ~ ~ / / / . . . / / / / / / / / /
        ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ / / / . . . / / / / / . P / /
      ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ / / / . . . / / / / / P P / /
      ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ / / / . . . / / / / / . . / /
      X ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ / / / . . L / / / / / / / / /
      ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ / / / . . . / / / / / / / / /
      ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ / / / . . . / / / / / / / / /
      ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ / / / . . . / / / / / / / / /
        ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ / / / . . . / / / / / / / / /
          ~ ~ ~ ~ / / / . . . / / / / / / / / /

  . safe   / too steep   ~ water   P person   ? unsure   (blank) not seen
  L where the fail-safe chose to land now   X where the drone came down

At night the creek is warmer than the land. Read with the daytime rule, where water is the
cooler surface, 0 of the 58 patches judged to be water would still read as water.

When the fail-safe chose to land, at 69 s, the ground below was safe. The descent took 22 s,
and the night wind carried the drone 44 m downhill before it touched down.
The controller knew the ground, and nothing in it acted on what it knew. Part 3 adds action.

Faults and fail-safes from the controller's log:
  t=50 s: Satellite fix degraded: 7 satellites, HDOP 3.5.
  t=57 s: Satellite fix lost: none for 3 s.
  t=57 s: Fail-safe set to hold: no satellite fix, but the link is up, so wait for the fix or the operator.
  t=69 s: Command link lost: 95 % of packets dropped for 5 s.
  t=69 s: Fail-safe set to land now: with no satellite fix and no link, the drone can neither navigate nor receive orders.
  t=85 s: Battery critical: one cell at 3.25 V.`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Part 2: Dynamic context — DeepCausality"
  description="Part 2 of the drone fail-safe tutorial: a thermal camera and a LiDAR fill a context with uncertain readings of the ground, and the controller judges every patch from it."
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <article class="static-page">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">Dynamic drone fail-safe · Part 2 of 5</p>
      <h1>Part 2: Dynamic context</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A thermal camera and a LiDAR now look down every second, and the controller fuses what
        they see into a context. From the context it judges every patch of ground: safe, too
        steep, water, a person, or unsure.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Run it</h2>
      <Code code={run} lang="bash" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        The source lives in <a href={source}><code>part_2_dynamic_context</code></a>.
      </p>

      <h2>The ground as a context</h2>
      <p>
        Each second the camera and the LiDAR read every 4 m patch of ground in their footprint,
        which shrinks as the drone descends. Each reading gives five quantities: the patch's mean
        temperature, its hottest spot, its slope, the fraction of LiDAR beams that return, and how
        far anything sticks up from it. Every reading is noisy, and noisier the higher the drone
        flies.
      </p>
      <p>
        The controller keeps the ground in a <a href="/overview/dynamic-context/">context</a>:
      </p>
      <Code code={contextType} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        A patch seen for the first time gets a space node at its center and five data nodes, one
        per quantity, each linked to the space node. Every later frame updates the data nodes:
      </p>
      <Code code={fuse} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        A data node holds an uncertain value. The controller fuses every frame that has seen the
        patch, weighting each reading by its inverse variance, so the value sharpens with each look:
      </p>
      <Code code={dataNode} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />

      <h2>Two new stages</h2>
      <p>
        The causal process gains two stages between <code>sense</code> and <code>detect</code>:
      </p>
      <Code code={stages} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        <code>perceive</code> sets the context's clock to the local time and fuses the frame into
        the context. <code>judge</code> reads each patch back from the context and judges it. The
        ladder in <code>decide</code> stays the one from part 1.
      </p>

      <h2>Judging a patch</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Too steep:</strong> the slope exceeds 15°, where a drone on skids tips over.</li>
        <li><strong>Water:</strong> the LiDAR finds no surface, and the patch reads more than 2 °C warmer than the land at night, or cooler by day. The clock node says which.</li>
        <li><strong>A person:</strong> a hot spot 15 °C above the land, or a bump over 1 m where the LiDAR finds a surface.</li>
        <li><strong>Safe:</strong> none of these, and the LiDAR finds a surface.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Each test compares uncertain values and yields an uncertain Boolean:
      </p>
      <Code code={judge} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />
      <p>
        A sequential probability test decides each one at 95 % confidence. It stops drawing samples
        once the evidence settles the question, and its seeded draws make every run judge alike.
        A patch neither safe nor a hazard at that confidence reads as unsure.
      </p>
      <Code code={holds} lang="rust" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} wrap />

      <h2>What happened</h2>
      <Code code={output} lang="text" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
      <p>
        The controller now knows the ground. It found the first tower pad at 18 s, the crew on the
        second pad, the flat terrace below it when it chose to land, and the creek it drifted into.
        The map shows the terrace as a strip of safe patches in the steep grass.
      </p>
      <p>
        The time of day matters. The daytime rule expects water cooler than the land; read with
        it, the same context finds no water in the creek.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fail-safe still lands the drone in the creek. The controller knows the ground, and
        nothing in it acts on what it knows. <a href={`${DRONE_FAILSAFE_BASE}dynamic-action/`}>Part 3</a> adds action.
      </p>
      <p>
        The simulation places every frame in world coordinates, so a patch keeps its place while
        the drone drifts without GPS. A real system registers frames against each other with
        visual or LiDAR odometry.
      </p>

      <TopicList label="Dynamic drone fail-safe" current="dynamic-context" items={droneFailsafeParts} base={DRONE_FAILSAFE_BASE} />
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
